+//
+// One rpc_peer_hub per server PROCESS (not per client connection -- rpc_serve_client() below
+// constructs a fresh rpc_server for every accepted client, but pushes and their matching WAITs
+// can straddle client connections in principle, and definitely straddle the "one command loop at
+// a time" structure of a single connection, so this state has to outlive any one rpc_server).
+// Holds:
+//  - the inbox: pushes that have arrived via RPC_CMD_PUSH_TENSOR (from this server's peer
+//    listener, i.e. from another server) but have not yet been claimed by a client's
+//    RPC_CMD_WAIT_INBOUND. A push can arrive before its WAIT does -- the client only knows to
+//    send WAIT after it has already told the source server to FORWARD, and the source server may
+//    finish the push before the client's WAIT reaches this server -- so this has to be a proper
+//    inbox (store-then-claim), not a rendezvous that assumes WAIT is already waiting.
+//  - outbound peer connections: lazily opened, persistent client sockets to the peer servers
+//    named on this server's --peer command line, used by forward_tensor() to relay pushes.
